# Untitled

Improving performance of \(\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{A g B i B r}_{6}\) solar cell through constructing gradient energy level with deep-level hole transport material
Abstract The valence band offset between \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) and hole transport layer (HTL) is approximately \(1.00~\mathrm{eV}\), which results in high energy loss and is identified as one of the bottle necks of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) perovskite solar cell (PSC) for achieving high power conversion efficiency (PCE). To tackle this problem, we propose the optimization of the energy level alignment by designing and synthesizing novel deep-level hole transport materials (HTMs). The sole introduction of deep-level HTMs successfully reduces the valence band offset between \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) and HTL, but induces the increased valence band offset at HTL/Au interface, limiting the PCE improvement. To further solve the problem and improve the PCE, the gradient energy level arrangement is constructed by combining the newly developed deep-level HTM \(6{,}6^{\circ}\) -(3-((9,9-dimethyl-9H-fluoren-3-yl)(4-methoxyphenyl)amino)thiophene-2,5-diyl)bis (N-(9,9-dimethyl-9H-fluoren-2-yl)-N,9-bis(4-methoxyphenyl)-9H-carbazol-3-amine) (TF) with \(^{2,2^{\circ},7,7^{\circ}}\) -tetrakis \((N,N^{\prime}\) -dipmethoxyphenylamine)-9,9-spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD). Through optimization, an impressive PCE of \(3.50\%\) with remarkably high open-circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\) and fill factor
(FF) is achieved, qualifying it among the best pristine \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) PSCs.
Keywords All-inorganic \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) perovskite; Stepped energy level structure; Energy loss; Moisture resistance
## 1 Introduction
Recently, organic-inorganic hybrid lead-halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have made impressive developments and received extensive worldwide attention [1–5]. The record certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of single-junction PSC has reached \(25.7\%\) [6], making PSC a promising next-generation photovoltaic technology [7–11]. Nevertheless, the volatilization of organic cations [12, 13] and the toxicity of lead [14–16] hindered the commercial application of PSCs. Therefore, it is highly desirable to develop efficient all-inorganic lead-free PSCs [17–19].
At present, most of researches on lead-free PSCs focus on the replacement of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) with its homolog elements, such as tin \((\mathrm{Sn}^{2+})\) and germanium \((\mathrm{Ge}^{2+})\) [20, 21]. However, \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ge}^{2+}\) are easily oxidized to \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ge}^{4+}\) in ambient condition, resulting in poor stability [19, 22]. Besides \(\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}\) or \(\mathrm{Ge}^{2+}\)-based lead-free perovskite materials, all-inorganic double perovskite material \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\), which is thermal stable and eco-friendly, was proposed as a potential material for constructing efficient and stable lead-free PSCs [23]. To date, the highest PCE ever achieved by pristine \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) is only \(3.31\%\), which is far from the theoretical limit efficiency of \(8\%\) [24]. The research manifests that the key restricting factors for the performance of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) PSCs are the wide energy gap \((E_{\mathrm{g}},\,2.18\ \mathrm{eV})\)
[25], poor film forming feature, easily forming defects and mismatched energy level alignment [26]. Most recently, great efforts are devoted to extending the spectral response of \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\)-based PSC [27, 28] and regulating precursor solution additive [29] and crystallization kinetics of \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) film [30], achieving huge improvement of PCE (see Table S1) while the optimization of device energy level alignment is rarely reported and lags behind.
Notably, in most of efficient \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) PSCs, doped \(2,2',7,7'\)-tetrakis(N,N'-dipmethoxyphenylamine)-9,9-spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD) was commonly used as the hole transport layer (HTL) [31, 32]. The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) of Spiro-OMeTAD is approximately \(-5.20\ \mathrm{eV}\) [33], and the valence band (VB) of \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\), as measured by ultraviolet (UV) photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS), is \(-6.22\ \mathrm{eV}\) (see Figs. S1 and S2). The energy potential difference between the HOMO level of Spiro-OMeTAD and the VB of \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) is close to \(1\ \mathrm{eV}\). Theoretically, to ensure the efficient hole extraction and transport, the driven force should be larger than \(0.2\ \mathrm{eV}\), but excessive driven force will cause extra energy loss, resulting in lower open-circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\) [34]. Therefore, it makes sense to tune the energy level alignment at \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\)/HTL interface. One strategy is adjusting the energy level of \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) by employing bulk doping or surface modification method [35, 36]. For instance, by forming 2D \((\mathrm{PEA})_{4}\mathrm{AgBiBr_{6}}\) perovskite on the 3D \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) surface (PEA is phenylethylamine), Sirtl et al. [32] successfully shifted the VB of the perovskite film toward the HOMO of Spiro-OMeTAD, with \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) increasing from 1.07 to \(1.18\ \mathrm{V}\). The other strategy is introduction of hole transport materials (HTMs) with deep-lying HOMO levels to minimize the energy loss. To fulfill this strategy, it is urgently desirable to develop and search novel HTMs with deep HOMO levels, high hole mobile and conductivity.
In this context, by selecting thiophene and carbazole with relatively weak electron donating ability as core building block and \(\pi\)-bridge, together with N-(4-methoxyphenyl)-9,9-dimethyl-9H-fluoren-2-amine as peripheral capping group, we reported two new small molecular materials, termed as DF and TF, with deep HOMO levels, high hole mobility and hole conductivity. These features make DF and TF promising HTMs for \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) PSCs. Applied as dopant-free HTMs, both DF and TF achieved comparable even higher PCEs than doped Spiro-OMeTAD. Compared with the reference device, it was found that the valence band offset between \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) and HTL effectively reduced with the introduction of DF or TF as HTM, but the valence band offset between HTL and Au electrode increased, limiting the PCE. To tackle this problem, we tried to construct cascaded binary HTL system by combining DF or TF with Spiro-OMeTAD, with the aim of
synergistically reducing the energy loss in both \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\)/HTL and HTL/Au interfaces. Through optimization, the \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) PSC based on TF/Spiro-OMeTAD binary HTL achieved an impressive PCE of \(3.50\%\), which is the highest efficiency ever reported for pristine \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) PSC without extending absorption (see Table S1). Furthermore, the existence of hydrophobic dopant-free TF film on \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) inhibits the erosion of perovskite by water moisture. After \(1500\ \mathrm{h}\) aging, the unencapsulated devices stored in the ambient environment \((25{-}30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\ 45\%{-}60\%\) RH) still retain over \(90\%\) of the initial efficiency. Our research paves a new way to construct efficient and stable \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) PSCs.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Instrumentation
\({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) and \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR spectra were recorded on a Bruker AM 400 spectrometer with the chemical shifts against tetramethylsilane (TMS), operating at 400 and \(100\ \mathrm{MHz}\), respectively. High-resolution mass spectrometry (HRMS) results were obtained with a Thermo Scientific Combined Quadrupole Mass Spectrometer. UPS was performed by PHI 5000 VersaProbe III with He I source \((21.22\ \mathrm{eV})\) under an applied negative bias of \(9.0\ \mathrm{V}\).
## 2.2 Sample fabrication
The fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) substrates were sequentially washed with deionized water, ethanol, and acetone for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) with sonication. The electron transporting \(\mathrm{c-TiO_{2}}\) layer was coated by spray-coating technique and annealed at \(500\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(120\ \mathrm{min}\). The substrates were treated with oxygen plasma for \(20\ \mathrm{min}\). Second, the \(\mathrm{m-TiO_{2}}\) layer was deposited by spin-coating the \(\mathrm{TiO_{2}}\) paste (30NR-D)/ethanol mixture \((1:3\ \mathrm{w/w})\) at \(4000\ \mathrm{r\cdot min^{-1}}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{s}\) on the surface of \(\mathrm{c-TiO_{2}}\), which was then calcined at \(300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(45\ \mathrm{min}\). After that, the \(\mathrm{Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}}\) precursor solution was synthesized by dissolving \(0.2554\ \mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{CsBr}\), \(0.1127\ \mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{AgBr}\), and \(0.2692\ \mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathrm{BiBr_{3}}\) in \(1\ \mathrm{ml}\) of dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) with being stirred at \(100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) until the salts were fully dissolved. The prepared substrates were also treated with oxygen plasma as described previously. The perovskite layer was spin-coated onto the FTO/ \(\mathrm{c-TiO_{2}/m-TiO_{2}}\) substrates at \(3000\ \mathrm{r\cdot min^{-1}}\) for \(60\ \mathrm{s}\) and \(200\ \upmu\mathrm{l}\) isopropanol anti-solvent was applied dropwise for the last \(25\ \mathrm{s}\) during the spin-coating. The film was annealed at \(280\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5\ \mathrm{min}\). All the above experimental procedures were carried out in a glove box. The substrate with above layers were placed to the dry glove box to prepare the hole transfer layer. \(10\ \mathrm{mg\cdot ml^{-1}}\) of DF-chlorobenzene (CB)
solution and \(10~\mathrm{mg\cdot ml}^{-1}\) of TF-CB solution were prepared. The DF-CB/TF-CB solution was spin-coated on the perovskite layer at \(5000~\mathrm{r\cdot min}^{-1}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{~s~}\). The film was annealed at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\). A CB solution \(\left(1\,\mathrm{\ml}\right)\) of Spiro-OMeTAD \((80~\mathrm{mg})\) with a \(15.5~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulphonyl)imide (Li-TFSI) solution \((510~\mathrm{mg\cdot ml}^{-1}\) in acetonitrile) and \(10.5~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) 4-tert-butylpyridine (TBP) was then spin-coated on the TF film. Finally, a \(100{\-}\mathrm{nm}\) -thick gold electrode was vapour deposited on the top layer by an organic evaporation coater to complete the cell. The effective area of \(20\,\mathrm{\mm}^{2}\) of the electrode was controlled by the mask plate. It should be noted that the Spiro-OMeTAD hole transfer layer needs to be placed in a moisture-proof oven for \(12~\mathrm{h}\) before vapour deposition.
## 3 Results and discussion
The detailed molecular structures of DF and TF are listed in Fig. 1a, with the detailed synthesis routes and characterization shown in Supporting Information (SI). Interestingly, DF and TF can be simultaneously obtained from the same reaction. By increasing reaction temperature and time, the yield of TF can be improved (see Table S2), which may be caused by the higher energy required for coupling the third \(N\!\cdot\) -(4-methoxyphenyl)-9,9-dimethyl-9Hfluoren-2-amine on top of DF. Under the optimized conditions, the total yields of DF and TF can be as high as \(90\%\) or more.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculation results (Figs. 1b and S3) show that DF exhibits nearly planar backbone. With the introduction of additional N-(4-methoxyphenyl)-9,9-dimethyl-9H-fluoren-2-amine on the 3-site of thiophene core of DF, the resulted TF molecule shows the orthogonal 3D configuration, which can effectively inhibit the aggregation of molecules and beneficial to the formation of amorphous film [37, 38]. For DF and TF, the electrons delocalize on the entire molecular skeleton at the HOMO state and move toward the central parts at the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) state. The higher overlapping extent of electron cloud density at HOMO and LUMO states for TF suggests the stronger intermolecular Coulombic forces, which facilitates the extraction and transport of photogenerated holes [39–41]. The HOMO and LUMO energy levels are calculated to be \(-4.38\) and \(-0.89~\mathrm{eV}\) for DF and \(-4.42\) and \(-0.76~\mathrm{eV}\) for TF, respectively. The introduction of the third aniline fluorene group on the 3-site of thiophene core weakens the electron-donating ability of the entire molecule, thus reducing the HOMO level of TF. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) and UPS measurements (Figs. S4, S5, Table S3) confirm the change tendency. In film state, DF and TF show HOMO energy levels of \(-5.44\) and \(-5.46~\mathrm{eV}\), respectively, which are about \(0.26{-}0.28~\mathrm{eV}\) more negative than Spiro-OMeTAD. Therefore, by using DF and TF as HTMs, the energy loss between \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) and HTL can be reduced, and higher \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) can be expected. The hole mobility of Spiro-OMeTAD, DF, and TF is tested to be
\(6.82 \times 10^{-4}\), \(7.86 \times 10^{-4}\) and \(9.61 \times 10^{-4} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \cdot \mathrm{V}^{-1} \cdot \mathrm{s}^{-1}\), respectively (see Fig. S6, Table S3). Compared with DF, TF possesses higher hole mobility, which is mainly due to the additional hole transport channels with the introduction of the third peripheral substituent [42].
Considering the suitable energy level, high hole mobility and conductivity, we tried to use the newly developed material as dopant-free HTM in \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) PSCs, and Spiro-OMeTAD was selected as the reference HTM to better evaluate these two HTMs [43, 44]. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) showed that dense and smooth \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) film could be produced using one-step method, which is essential for achieving high efficiency (Fig. 2a). Meanwhile, the time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (ToF-SIMS) depth profile and 3D images of perovskite films indicate that Cs ions are evenly distributed, which further confirms the excellent film quality (see Fig. 2b). Dopant-free DF- and TF-based devices obtained PCEs of \(2.44\%\) and \(2.66\%\) (Fig. 2c and Table S4), respectively, which are overperformed that of Spiro-OMeTAD-based reference device \((2.32\%)\) under the same condition. Especially, the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and short-circuit current \((J_{\mathrm{sc}})\) were improved. All the devices showed good reproducibility, as shown in Fig. S7. The improvement of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\), on the one hand, can be mainly ascribed to the decreased
trap defect density of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) film with the introduction of DF and TF as HTMs (see Fig. S8). And on the other hand, it can be attributed to the reduction of energy loss between \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}/\mathrm{HTL}\) interface. According to the incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra of PSCs based on different HTMs, it was found that the DF- and TF-based PSCs got higher IPCE values in \(420{-}450\ \mathrm{nm}\) region. The longer wavelength light possesses deeper incident depth, generating more holes near the HTL/perovskite interface [45]. The higher hole mobility of HTMs will facilitate the extraction and transport of holes near the HTL. Therefore, for DF- and TF-based devices, slightly higher IPCE values were obtained in \(420{-}450\ \mathrm{nm}\) region, which explains well the higher \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\).
Compared with the Spiro-OMeTAD-based reference device, although the valence band offset between \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) and HTL is reduced and higher \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) is achieved, the charge transport driven force between HTL and Au electrode is enlarged, which may cause overmuch energy loss and become another salient issue. Given that the two new materials DF and TF can form gradient level arrangement with Spiro-OMeTAD, inserting Spiro-OMeTAD between DF (or TF)-based HTL and Au, both of the energy loss at \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}/\mathrm{HTL}\) and HTL/Au interfaces might be well restricted [46]. In order to ensure the successful
implementation of this strategy, it is requisite that DF and TF can tolerate chlorobenzene, which will be used for the following Spiro-OMeTAD deposition. X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS) measurements were firstly conducted to preliminarily exam the chlorobenzene tolerance of DF and TF. The strong N 1s signal (see Fig. S9) indicates that DF and TF still exist after washing with CB, ensuring the possibility of introducing them as interface layer between \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) and Spiro-OMeTAD. The stronger signal for TF indicates the better tolerance of TF. As further confirmed by SEM and atomic force microscope (AFM) measurements (Figs. S10, S11), TF film still keeps continuous and dense after being washed with CB. While for DF film, the aggregated dots are washed away, generating lots of pin holes and resulting in larger roughness. More importantly, the appearance of TF underlayer facilitates the formation of flat and dense Spiro-OMeTAD film (Figs. S12, S13), which is essential to achieve high PCE. The ToF-SIMS technique was employed to understand the spatial distribution of DF or TF in final binary HTL. The side view of ToF-SIMS depth profile and 3D image confirmed that TF partly mixed with Spiro-OMeTAD, and the thickness of the TF underlayer is around \(6.7~\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 3a).
Following that, devices with the configuration of \(\mathrm{FTO/c-TiO_{2}/m-TiO_{2}/Cs_{2}AgBiBr_{6}/DF}\) (or TF) interlayer/Spiro-OMeTAD/Au were prepared to verify the feasibility of the strategy. As shown in Fig. 3a, the fabricated PSC shows well layer-by-layer structure, with the thickness of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) film and HTL measured to be 600 and 100 nm, respectively. The device showed well cascaded energy level alignment as shown in Fig. 3b. Encouragingly, the \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) PSCs containing DF or TF interlayer indeed obtained higher PCEs than reference device (see Fig. 3c, Table S5), though DF tends to be partly dissolved by CB. The device based on TF interlayer achieved a champion PCE of \(3.50\%\) , with the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(1.19\;\mathrm{~V~}\) , a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of 3.87 \(\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and a notable fill factor (FF) of \(75.9\%\) . The integrated \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) calculated from IPCE spectra well matched with the \(J_{-}V\) experimental data (Fig. 3d), further verifying the reliability of the \(J_{-}V\) curves. According to the statistics of PCE distribution of the device, the interlayer has good reproducibility in improving the device performance (see Fig. 3e). The steady-state output of PSCs with or without TF interlayer showed that both devices could steadily work at maximum power point (MPP) (see Fig. 3f) during measurement.
The greatly improved \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) is one of the most prominent reasons for the enhanced PCE. To well illustrate the reason, the built-in potentials \((V_{\mathrm{bi}})\) of the PSCs based on different HTLs are tested [47, 48]. The sample containing TF as interlayer shows the highest \(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\) value of \(0.94\,\mathrm{~V~}\) (see Fig. 4a), suggesting the mostly suppressed charge recombination at \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}/\mathrm{HTL}\) interface with the insertion of
TF. The PSC containing TF as interlayer also shows the largest recombination resistance \((R_{\mathrm{rec}})\) and the lowest leakage current (see Fig. 4c, d), further confirming the suppressed charge recombination with the optimization of energy level alignment. As stated above, with the introduction of DF or TF on top surface of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) film, the trap density only slightly decreases (Fig. 4b), which is a secondary reason for suppressed charge recombination. The main reason should be the enhanced hole extraction and transport with introduction of TF as interlayer, as confirmed by the steady-state PL and time-resolved PL (TRPL) measurements (Figs. 4e, f, S14 and Table S6). With the deposition of HTMs on top of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) film, higher PL quenching efficiency and decreased carrier lifetime \((\tau_{\mathrm{avg}})\) value means higher hole extraction efficiency and faster hole extraction rate [49, 50]. The TF: Spiro-OMeTAD-based sample showed the highest quenching efficiency and lowest decay lifetime, indicating the most efficient hole transport efficiency, which was consistent with the \(J_{-}V\) results.
With the inserting of dopant-free TF which is hydrophobic, between \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) and Spiro-OMeTAD, not only the hydrophobicity of HTL was slightly enhanced, but also TF film can act as a barrier to further prevent water vapor from damaging the \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) film (see Figs. 5a and S15). The effect of the inserting layer on device long-term stability was further evaluated. The unencapsulated PSCs were kept at ambient condition \((25{-}30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},45\%{-}60\%\) RH). As shown in Fig. 5b, the reference device kept stable during the initial \(400\,\mathrm{h}\) , but the PCE quickly dropped to around \(30\%\) of its initial after \(1500\,\mathrm{~h~}\) aging. In comparison, with the introduction of TF as interlayer, the corresponding PSCs showed significantly enhanced stability and kept working with \(90\%\) of its initial PCE after \(1500\ \mathrm{h}\) aging. The volatility of TBP and hydrophilic Li-TFSI are considered to be the main reasons for accelerating the degradation of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) PSCs, and the insertion of TF layer is helpful to improve the stability of \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) PSC.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, to minimize the \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) loss and achieve higher efficiency, we proposed to optimize the energy level alignment at \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}/\mathrm{HTL}\) interface by using newly developed HTMs DF and TF with deep-lying HOMO levels. The research manifested that sole introduction of HTMs with deep-lying HOMO levels can reduce the energy loss between \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) and HTL, but induce the increased valence band offset at HTL/Au interface, limiting the PCE improvement. Considering that the two new materials DF and TF can form gradient level arrangement
with Spiro-OMeTAD, and to further tackle this problem at HTL/Au interface, we further tried to construct binary HTL with cascaded energy level alignment. It is gratifying that a PCE of \(3.50\%\) with remarkably higher \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) was achieved by
employing TF: Spiro-OMeTAD-based binary HTL due to the enhanced hole extraction and transport capability of HTL and the well-restricted charge recombination. Moreover, with the introduction of the dopant-free TF interlayer,
the degradation of \( \mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6} \) by moisture was also well restricted, leading to better long-term stability. The research paves a new way to fabricate highly efficient and stable \( \mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6} \) PSCs.
